   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_5YS8DBzhKYhKkZ83",
   "name": "Acids, Bases & Protons",
   "definition": "understand acids and bases in terms of proton transfer\r\nunderstand that an acid is a proton donor and a base is a proton acceptor"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Acids, bases & protons"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "Proton transfer"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The earlier definition of an acid and a base can be extended\n- In terms of proton transfer, we can further define each substance in how they interact with protons"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "Acids"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Acids are **proton donors **as they ionize in solution producing protons, H<sup>+</sup> ions\n- These H<sup>+</sup> ions make the aqueous solution acidic\n- **Example: **hydrochloric acid"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "**HCl (aq)   →    H**<sup>**+**</sup>** (aq)    +    Cl**<sup>**–**</sup>** (aq)**"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "Bases (Alkalis)"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Bases (alkalis) are** proton acceptors** as they ionize in solution producing OH<sup>-</sup> ions which can accept protons\n- These OH<sup>-</sup> ions make the aqueous solution alkaline\n- **Example: **sodium hydroxide"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "**NaOH (s)   →    Na**<sup>**+**</sup>** (aq)    +    OH**<sup>**–**</sup>** (aq)**"
  }
 ]
}